## Why This Is Asked

- Demonstrates ability to replace branches with bit-twiddling for performance.
- Applies a well-known, portable technique to a common memory-allocation problem.
- Handles edge cases and overflow without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix-fill trick to propagate the highest set bit.
- Branchless arithmetic to compute next power of two.
- Overflow behavior on 32-bit boundaries.

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t nextPowerOfTwo32(uint32_t n) {
  // Assumes n > 0. No explicit branches.
  n--;
  n |= n >> 1;
  n |= n >> 2;
  n |= n >> 4;
  n |= n >> 8;
  n |= n >> 16;
  return n + 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

